# Wingspan .env — a CLI/dev-only convenience. wingspan-cli and
# wingspan-devserver read `./.env` from their working directory at startup;
# the desktop app never reads this file at all. Its Schwab credentials come
# from whatever `[schwab] env_file` names instead, and even that one it only
# ever PARSES for the values it holds — it is never exported into the
# desktop app's own process environment.
# A variable already set in the real environment ALWAYS wins over this file.
# Any WINGSPAN_* override works here (they beat wingspan.toml), which is how
# the two production pointers below work.

# 1) The env file (owned by your other Schwab tooling) holding
#    SCHWAB_APP_KEY and SCHWAB_APP_SECRET:
WINGSPAN_SCHWAB__ENV_FILE=/path/to/schwab-secrets.env

# 2) The TOML config that points at the Schwab token JSON, e.g.
#      [paths]
#      token_file = "/home/you/.tokens/schwab_token.json"
#    The token itself is produced and refreshed by that other tooling.
#    Wingspan only needs to READ it: it never writes, moves, chmods, copies
#    or backs up the file, and a refreshed access token stays in memory.
WINGSPAN_SCHWAB__TOKEN_CONFIG=/path/to/schwab-paths.toml
# If your file uses a different table/key, name it (dotted):
# WINGSPAN_SCHWAB__TOKEN_CONFIG_KEY=paths.token_file

# Anything else works too, e.g. (wingspan-cli/wingspan-devserver only —
# the desktop app ignores this file entirely):
# WINGSPAN_TRADES__ROOT=~/Trades
# SCHWAB_APP_KEY=only-if-you-must   # prefer the env_file indirection
